"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,365\nEpisode 7\n\n3\n00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:09,212\nSugar highs\n\n4\n00:00:12,012 --> 00:00:14,345\nA birthday party, a pile of cake,\n\n5\n00:00:14,485 --> 00:00:17,502\nand a room full of children bouncing off the walls.\n\n6\n00:00:17,875 --> 00:00:19,740\nEveryone has seen a sugar high.\n\n7\n00:00:20,016 --> 00:00:20,934\nOr have they?\n\n8\n00:00:21,104 --> 00:00:25,834\nThe claim: sugar makes children hyperactive and unable to focus.\n\n9\n00:00:26,213 --> 00:00:29,194\nIt is one of the most widely believed brain myths,\n\n10\n00:00:29,395 --> 00:00:31,324\namong parents and teachers alike.\n\n11\n00:00:31,494 --> 00:00:40,204\nIn 1995, Mark Wolraich and colleagues pooled twenty three double blind studies in the Journal of the American Medical Association.\n\n12\n00:00:40,739 --> 00:00:44,497\nChildren got either sugar or a sugar free placebo,\n\n13\n00:00:44,785 --> 00:00:46,708\nand no one watching knew which.\n\n14\n00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:52,764\nThe conclusion: sugar did not affect children's behavior or cognitive performance.\n\n15\n00:00:52,934 --> 00:00:54,033\nSo why do we see it?\n\n16\n00:00:54,472 --> 00:00:57,246\nA 1994 study offers a clue.\n\n17\n00:00:57,773 --> 00:01:01,825\nThirty five boys, described by their mothers as sugar sensitive,\n\n18\n00:01:02,089 --> 00:01:04,141\nall received a sugar free drink.\n\n19\n00:01:04,749 --> 00:01:08,733\nMothers who were told their son had sugar rated him as more hyperactive,\n\n20\n00:01:09,048 --> 00:01:11,219\nand watched and criticized him more.\n\n21\n00:01:11,389 --> 00:01:13,458\nExpectations shape what we see.\n\n22\n00:01:13,744 --> 00:01:15,251\nAnd settings matter too.\n\n23\n00:01:15,669 --> 00:01:17,694\nParties and holidays are exciting,\n\n24\n00:01:17,714 --> 00:01:19,405\nwith or without the cake.\n\n25\n00:01:19,575 --> 00:01:20,554\nSome caution.\n\n26\n00:01:20,991 --> 00:01:23,289\nThe authors noted that a small effect,\n\n27\n00:01:23,309 --> 00:01:25,832\nor effects in a few sensitive children,\n\n28\n00:01:25,844 --> 00:01:27,202\ncould not be ruled out.\n\n29\n00:01:27,639 --> 00:01:29,948\nAnd too much sugar still harms health.\n\n30\n00:01:30,259 --> 00:01:32,396\nIt just does not explain the party.\n\n31\n00:01:32,566 --> 00:01:34,856\nFor instructors, the lesson travels.\n\n32\n00:01:35,234 --> 00:01:38,304\nWhen we expect a student to struggle or misbehave,\n\n33\n00:01:38,512 --> 00:01:39,557\nwe tend to see it,\n\n34\n00:01:39,654 --> 00:01:41,486\nand we may treat them differently.\n\n35\n00:01:41,656 --> 00:01:42,833\nDo this instead.\n\n36\n00:01:43,214 --> 00:01:45,591\nBefore you explain a student's behavior,\n\n37\n00:01:45,611 --> 00:01:46,961\nlook at the situation,\n\n38\n00:01:46,981 --> 00:01:48,330\nnot a single cause.\n\n39\n00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:51,958\nNotice when your expectations are doing the observing.\n\n40\n00:01:52,236 --> 00:01:54,733\nAnd base judgments on evidence you can check.\n\n41\n00:01:55,248 --> 00:01:57,175\nOne tip to share with students: notice the setting,\n\n42\n00:01:57,406 --> 00:01:59,629\nnot just the snack.\n\n43\n00:01:59,799 --> 00:02:04,113\nSometimes the strongest effect in the room is what we expect to see.\n\n"